WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.090
A map app finds the fastest way

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.570
across a whole country in a blink.

3
00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.150
It doesn’t try every route:

4
00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:09.630
there are far too many.

5
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.620
So how does it know it found the shortest?

6
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.760
Today: shortest paths, the way

7
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.460
Dijkstra found them, in Python.

8
00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.100
Draw places as dots and roads as

9
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.120
lines, each with its length.

10
00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:28.470
In Python, a dictionary holds the

11
00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.510
map: each place points to its

12
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.590
neighbours and how far away they are.

13
00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:35.500
Dijkstra’s idea, from nineteen

14
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.300
fifty-six: give every place a number,

15
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.160
the shortest way found so far.

16
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:44.020
Home is zero; the others start very large.

17
00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:46.710
Each turn, settle the waiting

18
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.550
place with the smallest number.

19
00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.870
Then check its roads: can they make

20
00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.190
a neighbour’s number smaller?

21
00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.110
Why can you settle the smallest?

22
00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.350
Every other way to it would pass through a

23
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:01.330
place that is already further away.

24
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.640
With no negative road lengths,

25
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.500
nothing can ever make it shorter.

26
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.340
On our small map, home is zero.

27
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.610
Kesi gets one and Tala four.

28
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.180
Settle Kesi: through Kesi, Tala drops

29
00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.280
to three, and school gets eight.

30
00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.870
Settle Tala: school drops to six.

31
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.410
The straight-looking way, home to

32
00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.710
Tala to school, is seven.

33
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:29.980
Here it is in Python.

34
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.180
While places are waiting, take the one

35
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.520
with the smallest number, and remove it.

36
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.930
Then relax its roads: keep whichever is

37
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.350
smaller, the old number or the new way.

38
00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:42.970
Quick check!

39
00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:46.190
Two places are waiting: Tala at

40
00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.110
seven, and Rubi at five.

41
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.440
Which one do you settle next?

42
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.240
Rubi, the smallest.

43
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.340
Nothing can make its number smaller

44
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.920
now, because every other way passes

45
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.120
through places at seven or more.

46
00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.190
Settling Tala first could freeze a

47
00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.710
number that is still too big.

48
00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.250
In the lab’s map, the shortest way

49
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.070
to school is thirteen kilometres,

50
00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.810
through Tala, Kesi and Mina.

51
00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:15.990
The road that looks straightest,

52
00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:17.870
through Bora, is fifteen.

53
00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.060
Flood the bridge between Kesi and Mina,

54
00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.240
and it becomes fourteen, by Rubi and Sana.

55
00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.560
Shortest for whom?

56
00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.520
A new footbridge might save one village

57
00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.380
twenty minutes and another nothing.

58
00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.110
When a town chooses which road to

59
00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.670
fix, run the shortest paths from

60
00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.650
every home, and see who gains.

61
00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.660
In the app, open “The

62
00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.060
shortest way to school”.

63
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.270
Tap Home, then always the waiting

64
00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.030
village with the smallest number.

65
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.520
Then flood the bridge, and do it again.

66
00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:54.220
Your turn.

67
00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:57.070
A friend’s program settles places in

68
00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.150
list order, not smallest first.

69
00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.370
It says school is seven

70
00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:03.130
kilometres away, not six.

71
00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:04.400
Why?

72
00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:08.570
It settled Tala too early, before the

73
00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.210
shorter way through Kesi was known.

74
00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.070
Remember: store the map as a

75
00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.150
dictionary, settle the smallest

76
00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.970
number first, and relax its roads.

77
00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.560
And ask who a new road really helps.

78
00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:25.410
Try it yourself: draw the roads

79
00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.910
between your home, two friends’ homes

80
00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.210
and school, with rough distances.

81
00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.200
Run Dijkstra on paper, then

82
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.200
check it with your code.
